<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sunno</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 388</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sunno was a Frankish king of the late 4th century who, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomer">Marcomer</a>, raided Roman territory across the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 388. The Frankish general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbogast">Arbogast</a>, serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, campaigned against them in 392. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stilicho">Stilicho</a> sent Marcomer into exile, Sunno tried to avenge him but was killed by his own followers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sunno was a leader, or dux, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. His invasion of 388 came while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus">Magnus Maximus</a>, the usurper who held the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Roman Gaul</a>, was surrounded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>. The raid is known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, who cited the now lost work of Sulpicius Alexander. In that account <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomer">Marcomer</a>, Sunno and Genobaud crossed into the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a> and Belgia, broke through the limes, killed many people, laid waste the most fruitful lands and threw the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> into panic.</p><p>The main body of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> then went back over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> with their booty, while some stayed in the Belgian woods. The Roman generals Nanninus and Quintinus, hearing the news in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, attacked those who remained and killed many of them. Quintinus then crossed the Rhine to punish the Franks in their own country, but his army was surrounded and beaten: some soldiers drowned in the marshes, others were killed, and few made it back.</p><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus">Magnus Maximus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomer">Marcomer</a> and Sunno held a short meeting about the attacks with the Frank Arbogastes, a magister militum of the Roman army. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> gave hostages, as was usual, and Arbogastes returned to his winter quarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. A couple of years later, by then master of the western army, Arbogastes crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>; Marcomer was seen with the Chatti and Ampsivarii, but the two sides did not engage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The poet Claudian records that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> arrested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomer">Marcomer</a> and banished him to a villa in Tuscany. Sunno, his brother, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and tried to make himself leader of Marcomer's band, but he was killed by his own people.</p><p>The later Liber Historiae Francorum says that after Sunno's death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomer">Marcomer</a> proposed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> should live under one king and put forward his son Pharamond. Modern scholars such as Edward James do not accept this account as historical, since it makes Marcomer a son of the Trojan king Priam and Sunno a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antenor">Antenor</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sunno” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.23,11.84&amp;year=388" aria-label="Etruria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4837 661 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sunno"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5287 931h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5287 931h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42.23,11.84&amp;year=388">What was here in 388 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
